<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Licinius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 265–<a href="/years/325">325</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Licinius I, Valerius Licinianus Licinius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Licinius was Roman emperor from 308 to 324. In 313 he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> agreed the policy of religious toleration known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-milan">Edict of Milan</a>, and he went on to defeat Maximinus Daia and rule the eastern provinces. Rivalry with Constantine led to war; defeated at Adrianople and Chrysopolis, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, in 324, he surrendered and was executed in 325.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/licinius.jpg" alt="Licinius" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown, found in Ephesos · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Licinius,_Kunsthistorisches_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Licinius was born to a Dacian peasant family in Moesia Superior and became a close friend of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galerius">Galerius</a>, whom he accompanied on the Persian expedition in 298. In 307 Galerius sent him as an envoy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to seek an agreement with the usurper Maxentius, and left him in charge of the eastern provinces. On 11 November 308 Galerius raised him to the rank of Augustus in the West, with command of the Balkan provinces of Illyricum, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>. In 310 he inflicted a severe defeat on the Sarmatians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galerius">Galerius</a> died in May 311, Licinius agreed with Maximinus Daza to share the eastern provinces, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> and Bosporus as the dividing line. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a> defeated Maxentius at the Battle of the Milvian Bridge in October 312, Licinius and Constantine recognised each other as supreme emperors, Licinius in the East and Constantine in the West. In March 313 Licinius met Constantine at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and married his half-sister Flavia Julia Constantia. The marriage was the occasion for the jointly issued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-milan">Edict of Milan</a>, which reissued Galerius's edict of toleration and added the return of confiscated property to Christian congregations and exemption of the clergy from municipal duties.</p><p>Maximinus Daza took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and Heraclea while Licinius was away, but on 30 April 313 Licinius crushed his army at the Battle of Tzirallum, and Daza died in August. Licinius then hunted down and killed several relatives of the Tetrarchs, among them Daza's wife and two children and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>'s wife Prisca. A civil war with Constantine in 316 saw Licinius defeated at Cibalae on 8 October and again at Mardia, after which they were reconciled and Licinius had his co-emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tension rose again in 321 when Constantine crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in pursuit of raiders. In 324 Constantine defeated Licinius's fleet, then his army at Adrianople on 3 July, and finally Licinius at Chrysopolis on 18 September. Licinius was at first spared through the intervention of his wife and imprisoned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>, but was later executed; Constantine accused him of trying to raise troops among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, and had him hanged.</p><p>Constantine's propaganda portrayed Licinius as a supporter of paganism, which may be inaccurate: evidence suggests he was for a period a committed supporter of Christians, and he adopted Christian symbols for his armies. Eusebius of Caesarea, writing under Constantine, charges him with expelling Christians from the palace and ordering sacrifices. After his death his statues were thrown down and his laws abolished, an erasure now called damnatio memoriae.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Licinius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Constantine the Great, having defeated <b>Licinius</b> nearby in 324 CE, refounded Byzantium on a far larger scale and dedicated it as Constantinople in 330 CE, beginning the city's second life as an imperial capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Edict of Milan (313 CE), agreed by Constantine and <b>Licinius</b>, and Constantine's subsequent patronage of the Church turned a persecuted sect into the empire's dominant institution; its consequences extended across two millennia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Roman Empire the emperor Constantine, after his victory at the Milvian Bridge in 312, favoured the church; in 313 he and his co-emperor <b>Licinius</b> granted Christians freedom of worship, and in 325 he called the Council of Nicaea.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The outnumbered army of the Roman emperor <b>Licinius</b> routed his rival Maximinus Daia at Tzirallum, identified with modern Çorlu in Eastern Thrace.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-30">30 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In their second civil war, Emperor Constantine I soundly defeated his rival and former colleague <b>Licinius</b> at Adrianople in Thrace.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-3">3 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>At Nicomedia, Emperor <b>Licinius</b> issued the letter agreed with Constantine granting freedom of worship to Christians and all others.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-13">13 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Felix Romuliana, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.9,22.19&amp;year=304" aria-label="Felix Romuliana on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Licinius"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4520 3807h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4520 3807h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.9,22.19&amp;year=304">What was here in 304 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
